Rehash certificates and set file permissions A script I use in a certificate folder. It:
creates the OpenSSL hash links (&lt;hash&gt;.0) for the CA bundle and the certificate bundles, builds ssl-bundle.crt (certificate + CA bundle), makes certificates read-only (0444) and keys readable by the owner only (0400), gives the files to the web server user (default nginx, or the user/group you pass as arguments). Run it inside the certificate folder: ./rehash.sh [user] [group]
#!/bin/bash - uid=$1 gid=$2 uid=${uid:=&#34;nginx&#34;} gid=${gid:=$uid} /bin/rm *.0 ln -s ca-bundle.crt `openssl x509 -hash -noout -in ca-bundle.crt`.0 cat wildcard_example_com.crt &gt; ssl-bundle.crt echo &gt;&gt; ssl-bundle.crt cat ca-bundle.crt &gt;&gt; ssl-bundle.crt ln -s ssl-bundle.crt `openssl x509 -hash -noout -in ssl-bundle.crt`.0 if [ -f wildcard_project_example_com.crt ]; then cat wildcard_project_example_com.crt &gt; project-bundle.crt echo &gt;&gt; project-bundle.crt cat ca-bundle.crt &gt;&gt; project-bundle.crt ln -s project-bundle.crt `openssl x509 -hash -noout -in project-bundle.crt`.0 fi chmod 0444 *.crt chmod 0400 *.key chown $uid:$gid *.{crt,key}
